WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.022
Now calculate admission counts. The correlated scalar returns one count per patient.

00:00:06.022 --> 00:00:08.382
Look for counts greater than one.

00:00:08.382 --> 00:00:14.262
You could place the count directly in WHERE, but displaying it first lets you verify the numbers and

00:00:14.262 --> 00:00:18.317
understand the condition before combining it.

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:20.787
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:26.122
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:26.122 --> 00:00:30.882
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:30.882 --> 00:00:34.792
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:34.792 --> 00:00:42.122
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:42.122 --> 00:00:46.332
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:00:46.332 --> 00:00:50.970
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
